{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "Incident resolution performance / severity-and-speed profile of tickets",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "Is_Critical",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Flags severity, a core facet of characterizing how urgent/important an incident is and a plausible driver of resolution performance."
    },
    {
      "name": "Resolution_Speed",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Directly measures how quickly incidents are resolved; this is the strongest anchor for a resolution-performance focus."
    },
    {
      "name": "Is_Printer_Related",
      "supports_focus": false,
      "rationale": "A narrow topical/category flag that sits somewhat apart from a severity/speed focus; it points more toward a 'what kind of issue' theme, weakening single-focus coherence."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.5,
  "structure_quality": 0.5,
  "focus_actionability": 0.65,
  "rationale": "Two of the three columns (Is_Critical, Resolution_Speed) cohere around an incident-resolution-performance focus, pairing severity with speed in a reasonable predictor/outcome structure. However, Is_Printer_Related introduces a category-specific theme that is not clearly part of that focus, making the set read partly as a grab-bag rather than a tightly centred concept. The implied focus (resolution speed by severity) is genuinely actionable for an analyst, but the mixed signal and derived-flag granularity (binary criticality, one arbitrary category flag) keep coherence and structure at moderate levels rather than strong."
}

